"Used and edited",
"Chapter 21, Words to remember (‘Terminology’, ‘Term’) and Step 1 (last two paragraphs)",
"Reduced to “The study, collection and management of specialised terms” (key term) and “The study of how terms are collected, named and managed is called terminology (Cabré, 1999)” (Step 1). ‘Systematic’ and ‘by analysing concepts’ were in the first full version (the records) and were dropped in the beginner revision. A second sense was added: “also the set of terms used in one field”, and the second Arabic name {{المصطلحية}} was added to {{علم المصطلح}}. A separate key term ‘Term’ (“a word or expression that names a concept in a special field”) keeps the document’s link between terms and concepts. Reason: Simplified for beginners; the extra sense and Arabic name were added because the handbook uses ‘terminology’ for the set of terms of a field and {{المصطلحية}} is the other standard Arabic name (the expert review 0). The definition is now credited to Cabré (1999), a published source, instead of to the team document."
